pub(super) const SPEC: &str = "Write the complete issue specification after inspecting the repository and supplied context. Record assumptions, scope and observable acceptance criteria. Separate required headless code verification from installer packaging; do not invent distribution deliverables absent from the original user task. Include relevant headless checks when packaging behavior is affected. Save the specification in project memory and declare its artifact. Finish the specification without implementing or asking for approval; planning and review belong to downstream nodes. Use relevant skills only when enabled and available.";

pub(super) const ARCHITECT: &str = "Write a self-contained implementation plan from the specification and repository. Include file changes, meaningful TDD steps, exact verification commands and expected results. Inspect build scripts and targets; label required headless code checks separately from optional installer packaging and provide a supported headless alternative. Apply the standing installer packaging policy. Read referenced documents and source files using permitted inspection tools. Save the plan in project memory and declare its artifact. Do not implement, delegate or start another workflow; the builder executes inline after plan_review. Use relevant skills only when enabled and available.";

pub(super) const PLAN_REVIEW: &str = "Review the complete specification and plan against the issue and repository. Use native file tools or read-only inspection commands to read documents, source and enabled skill instructions. Check scope, acceptance criteria, architecture, TDD and executable verification. Apply the standing installer packaging policy: correct plans that turn optional installer packaging into a required gate for an ordinary code change. Do not modify files, run builds/tests, commit or delegate. Return findings in summary/data. Return data.approved=true only when approved; otherwise data.approved=false and status=blocked with concrete corrections.";

pub(super) const BUILDER: &str = "Implement the approved plan inline in the supplied workspace. Use relevant enabled skills, meaningful TDD and real code-level verification. Run the relevant tests, checks and headless build; record commands, actual results and limitations. Apply the standing installer packaging policy, including when an older plan or feedback demands a GUI-dependent installer. Preserve failed command evidence while distinguishing successful app compilation from unverified installer packaging. Correct your own deliverable using all review feedback. Save lasting reports and verification evidence in project memory and declare artifacts. Do not delegate, replace downstream reviewers, publish or perform remote Git operations; Factory manages those phases.";

pub(super) const REVIEW_CODE: &str = "Review the actual implementation diff, changed files and surrounding contracts for behavior, errors, security and demonstrable regressions. Use native file tools or read-only inspection commands, including git diff, to read source, artifacts and enabled skill instructions. Do not modify files, run builds/tests, commit or delegate. Return findings in summary/data with real file/line evidence. Return data.approved=true only when approved; otherwise data.approved=false and status=blocked with concrete corrections.";

pub(super) const REVIEW_TESTS: &str = "Review test coverage, assertions, edge cases and the builder's TDD evidence. Run relevant documented tests, checks and builds with isolated fixtures, never live accounts or services. Write access is only for generated results, caches and logs in the authorized workspace/memory. Do not modify source, tests, manifests, lockfiles or documentation; do not commit, delegate or install globally. Record commands, actual results and not_run checks with concrete reasons. Apply the standing installer packaging policy: judge required code evidence separately from optional distribution checks and do not reject solely for an environmental installer limitation. Actual code failures, verification of changed packaging behavior and explicitly requested installer deliverables remain required gates. Return data.approved=true only with the necessary verification evidenced; otherwise data.approved=false and status=blocked with concrete failures, missing evidence or required corrections.";

pub(super) const REVIEW_ARCHITECTURE: &str = "Review the actual implementation against the approved plan and existing architecture: boundaries, dependencies, contracts, persistence, migrations and compatibility. Use native file tools or read-only inspection commands to read source, artifacts and enabled skill instructions. Do not modify files, run builds/tests, commit or delegate. Avoid unrelated redesigns. Return findings in summary/data with concrete evidence. Return data.approved=true only when approved; otherwise data.approved=false and status=blocked with concrete corrections.";

pub(super) const PUBLISH_ISSUE: &str = "Check all required upstream approvals and verification evidence. Inspect the complete implementation diff, including untracked files, and commit only authorized changes locally. Do not reimplement or replace the reviews. Preserve documented non-blocking installer limitations in the PR body under the standing installer packaging policy; do not rerun GUI-dependent packaging. Return data.publish={title,body} to publish through Factory; body must include Closes #<issue number>. Factory resolves the repository's current default branch. Do not use gh or perform remote Git operations.";

pub(super) const APPROVED_PR: &str = "The pr_approved trigger already authorizes event.head_sha, including approval by an authorized OK comment; do not query GitHub reviews or comments to reconfirm approval. Check the approved PR against the authenticated refs/ai-factory/base and refs/ai-factory/head snapshot. If the snapshot says merged=true, return data.merge={method:'squash'} only to retry pending branch cleanup; do not modify files or resolve conflicts. Otherwise, if conflicts exist, merge the checked base into the current working branch, resolve conflicts preserving both changes, run relevant verification, and commit the correction locally. Return data.update_pr={} to update the existing PR branch through Factory and wait for new approval. Never combine update_pr with merge. If there are no conflicts and no local changes, return data.merge={method:'squash'} to merge through Factory. Leave version edits to the downstream version_bump node. If the assigned merge cannot be requested, return blocked with the concrete reason, never succeeded with only a report. Do not use gh or perform remote Git operations.";

pub(super) const REVIEW_PR: &str = "Review the PR diff and surrounding source using the authenticated refs/ai-factory/base and refs/ai-factory/head snapshot. Run relevant code-level tests and checks with isolated fixtures. Write access is only for generated results, caches and logs in the authorized workspace/memory. Do not modify source, tests, manifests, lockfiles or documentation; do not commit or delegate. Record checked SHAs, findings with file/line evidence, actual verification and limitations. Apply the standing installer packaging policy: documented optional packaging limitations alone do not require COMMENT or REQUEST_CHANGES when required code evidence passes. Return data.review={event:'APPROVE'|'REQUEST_CHANGES'|'COMMENT',body:'report'} to submit through Factory. APPROVE requires status=succeeded, complete required evidence and no blocking findings. REQUEST_CHANGES requires status=blocked and demonstrated required fixes. Missing required evidence requires COMMENT with status=blocked. Do not use gh or perform remote Git operations.";

pub(super) const VERSION_BUMP: &str = "Prepare the version bump after Factory has confirmed PR merge and source branch cleanup. Factory supplies a clean version workspace on the authenticated current default branch containing the approved merge. Use the current project version at base_sha as the bump starting point; merge_sha identifies only the approved PR diff, and other PRs may have advanced the branch. Inspect the actual merged PR diff (merge commit against its first parent), original PR refs, existing project version files and repository version conventions. Do not use the last release tag as the bump baseline: earlier merged PRs may already have bumped the version without tags. The highest-impact change determines the bump: major for incompatible changes, minor for backwards-compatible functionality, patch for compatible fixes or other maintenance changes. Explain concrete diff evidence and the version decision. If the merged PR already includes the required bump, preserve the current version without bumping again and leave HEAD unchanged, including when later PRs already advanced that version. Otherwise update all required project version files and lockfiles consistently, run relevant code-only verification, and create exactly one local version commit with the supplied GitHub identity. Do not change application behavior or unrelated files. Return data.bump_version={version:'X.Y.Z',bump:'major'|'minor'|'patch',reason:'diff evidence and version decision'} with status=succeeded. Factory publishes only the version commit to the default branch and records a receipt. Never create tags or GitHub releases, use gh, push, fetch, invoke external release CLIs, start another publish flow, merge again or request another approval. If the bump cannot be prepared, return blocked with a concrete reason and evidence. This assigned version node permits local version-file edits and a commit after merge; all remote-operation restrictions remain.";

pub(super) const LEGACY_RELEASE: &str = include_str!("release_prompt_legacy.txt");

pub(crate) fn release_prompt_override(
    flow_id: &str,
    node: &super::FlowNode,
) -> Option<&'static str> {
    (flow_id == "release" && node.id == "publish_app" && node.prompt == LEGACY_RELEASE)
        .then_some(RELEASE)
}

pub(crate) fn refresh_release_prompt(flow: &mut super::Flow) {
    for node in &mut flow.nodes {
        if let Some(prompt) = release_prompt_override(&flow.id, node) {
            node.prompt = prompt.to_owned();
        }
    }
}

pub const RELEASE: &str = r#"Publish the app requested by this [Release] issue using the project's configured distribution procedure and a suitable installed publishing skill available to the agent. Read that skill as the source of instructions; do not copy its pipeline into this prompt. Adapt or omit pipeline phases that are unnecessary for publishing this app. Execute inline without delegation, a new workflow, human checkpoints or execution-mode questions. Do not implement unrelated features or change versions: version changes belong only to the configured version_bump node after merge.
Before any distribution action, establish the target, artifacts, required configuration and authorized credentials from the issue, repository and publishing skill. If the publishing skill is unavailable, no suitable distribution target is configured, required credentials are missing, or the requested delivery cannot be verified within the current permissions and code-only policy, return blocked with the concrete missing prerequisite. Never claim publication from a source build alone.
Use the publishing skill within the release publication authorization supplied by Factory. Do not install skills or acquire broader permissions. Use gh and remote Git operations needed for the configured publication: inspect refs, create and push an existing-version tag, create a GitHub Release when required, and trigger and monitor GitHub Actions. Never force-push, overwrite or delete tags, push branches, merge PRs, create source/version commits or change versions. Reconcile existing remote tags and deployment runs before any write so retries reuse matching publication rather than duplicate it; block if a tag points to a different commit. For GitHub Actions procedures, inspect GitHub Actions secrets metadata and actual pipeline results; local process credentials are not required for steps that use repository/environment secrets. Factory action keys retain their existing restrictions: do not emit release, publish, update_pr, merge, review, comment or bump_version. Report direct publication evidence in descriptive data.
Keep all verification headless. For an explicitly requested distribution artifact verify the artifact and destination required by that procedure; if this requires GUI interaction or unavailable signing/distribution credentials, return blocked without running that path. Do not change OS security settings or packaging scripts to hide an environmental failure.
Return succeeded only after the authorized distribution procedure has completed and evidence confirms the requested publication. Save an evidence report in project memory and declare regular-file artifacts with SHA256. Return data.distribution_result.published=true only after verified publication. Factory closes the source Release issue as completed after all workflow steps succeed; do not close it yourself. Use descriptive node data such as verification and distribution_result; no reserved action keys. Preserve exact command outcomes, failed attempts, receipt/artifact evidence and concrete limitations without exposing secrets. This node is editable; its prompt, model, access and steps can be adapted to the project.
"#;
